WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.392
After the incident: postmortems that leave tests behind.

2
00:00:03.672 --> 00:00:19.651
Google’s SRE book describes blameless postmortems: written records of an incident, its impact, the actions taken, the contributing causes, and follow-up actions, written on the assumption that everyone acted in good faith with the information they had.

3
00:00:19.931 --> 00:00:24.880
Blame makes people hide problems, so the focus is fixing systems and processes.

4
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:39.411
It also recommends agreeing on triggers in advance, such as user-visible degradation past a threshold, any data loss, on-call intervention like a rollback, or a monitoring failure that meant a problem was found by hand.

5
00:00:39.691 --> 00:00:44.512
For LLM incidents, add three items to every postmortem’s action list.

6
00:00:44.792 --> 00:00:55.779
A test: the inputs that triggered the failure, and close variants, go into the eval set or the red-team set with the expected safe behavior, so the failure can’t return silently.

7
00:00:56.059 --> 00:01:03.269
A signal: the monitor or alert that would have caught it sooner, whether an online eval, a guard alert, or a cost threshold.

8
00:01:03.549 --> 00:01:09.373
A control: the guard, access check, or limit that was missing or failed, with an owner and a date.

9
00:01:09.653 --> 00:01:16.352
Then rerun the full eval suite against the fix, because a fix for one failure often shifts behavior elsewhere.

10
00:01:16.632 --> 00:01:30.541
Public incident collections, such as the AI Incident Database, are useful reading when you write your threat model: they show how real AI systems have failed in public, and which of those failures your own launch checklist would have caught.
